<p>Typical specifications from Hytrol, Dematic, and Intelligrated product lines. For built-in zone control without pneumatics, see <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a>. For <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">detailed pricing factors</a>, see the cost guide.</p>
<h2 id="zone-heading">Zone Control and Accumulation</h2>
<p>Standard powered roller conveyors run all rollers continuously. Adding zone control allows individual sections to start, stop, or change speed independently. Zone control converts a simple transport conveyor into an <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyor</a> that can buffer products without backpressure.</p>
<h3>BDLR Zone Methods</h3>
<p>Belt-driven live roller conveyors achieve zone control through two primary methods. Pneumatic belt lifters raise and lower sections of the drive belt to engage or disengage rollers in each zone. Pop-up stops hold products at zone boundaries while the belt continues running beneath disengaged rollers. Both methods require compressed air infrastructure, adding cost and maintenance.</p>
<h3>Line-Shaft Zone Methods</h3>
<p>Line-shaft conveyors achieve zone control through clutch/brake mechanisms on the line shaft or by using O-ring release assemblies. Some line-shaft designs use pneumatic clamps that press or release individual O-rings against the shaft. The inherent slip of O-rings provides a degree of passive accumulation even without active zone control.</p>
<h3>MDR Alternative</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (Motor Driven Roller) conveyors</a> provide zone control as a built-in feature without pneumatics, belt lifters, or clutch mechanisms. Each zone has its own 24VDC motor and photo-eye sensor. This makes MDR the preferred technology for new zero-pressure accumulation installations. MDR costs more per foot than BDLR or line-shaft but eliminates the compressed air infrastructure and mechanical zone control hardware.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Modes</h3>
<p>Powered roller conveyors support three accumulation modes when equipped with zone controls:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA):</strong> Products stop and queue with controlled gaps between them. No product-to-product contact. Protects fragile items from crushing and label scuffing.</li>
<li><strong>Minimum-pressure accumulation:</strong> Products queue with light contact (typically 2-5 lbs of backpressure). Lower cost than ZPA because fewer zones are needed. Acceptable for durable products like corrugated cases.</li>
<li><strong>Slug accumulation:</strong> Products accumulate in groups (slugs) with backpressure within each slug but gaps between slugs. Used for batch release applications like <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a> and palletizer layer forming.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Powered Roller Applications</h2>
<p><strong>End-of-line packaging:</strong> Powered roller conveyors connect case packers, case sealers, labelers, checkweighers, and palletizers in a continuous automated line. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">Packaging line integrators</a> use BDLR sections for high-speed case transport and accumulation zones before palletizers.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution and order fulfillment:</strong> Pick module systems use powered roller conveyors as trunk lines connecting pick zones to shipping. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> divert totes and cartons from trunk lines to pick stations and back. Dematic, Intelligrated, and TGW design large-scale distribution systems around powered roller and <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> conveyor platforms.</p>
<p><strong>Manufacturing:</strong> Assembly line applications use powered roller conveyors to move work-in-process between stations at a controlled pace. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyor</a> configurations stop and start the conveyor at precise intervals for robotic pick-and-place or manual operations.</p>
<p><strong>Food and beverage:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food-grade powered roller conveyors</a> use stainless steel frames, sealed bearings, and FDA-compliant belt materials. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary designs</a> feature tool-free belt removal for cleaning and sloped surfaces that prevent water pooling.</p>
<p><strong>Heavy-duty pallet handling:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> handle loaded pallets between stretch wrappers, <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet dispensers</a>, and shipping docks. Structural steel frames and heavy-duty bearings support loads from 1,000 to 10,000 lbs.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Choosing the Right Powered Roller</h2>
<p><strong>Choose BDLR when:</strong> you need medium-duty case transport (up to 500 lbs), speeds of 50-200 FPM, and can add pneumatic zone controls for accumulation. BDLR has the widest installed base and the most vendor options.</p>
<p><strong>Choose line-shaft when:</strong> you need light to medium-duty transport (up to 300 lbs), simple diverting (pop-up transfers work well with line-shaft), and light passive accumulation. Line-shaft excels where products must be easily diverted to side spurs using pop-up chain <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer units</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Choose CDLR when:</strong> product weight exceeds 500 lbs, you need positive drive with zero slip (such as <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyors</a>), or products have poor bottom surfaces that need high-torque roller contact.</p>
<p><strong>Choose MDR instead when:</strong> you need built-in zero-pressure accumulation, energy efficiency is a priority, low noise is critical, and product weight is under 250 lbs. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> cost more upfront but eliminate pneumatic infrastructure and reduce long-term energy and maintenance costs.</p>
<p>Many <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> combine multiple powered roller types. A typical system might use BDLR for high-speed transport trunk lines, MDR for accumulation zones, CDLR for pallet handling, and <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> for take-away sections.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is the difference between belt-driven and line-shaft?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR uses a flat belt under the rollers, friction between belt and roller creates rotation. Line-shaft uses a splined shaft with individual O-ring bands connecting each roller. BDLR provides higher torque (up to 500 lbs). Line-shaft provides smoother diverting and natural slip-clutch accumulation. See the full comparison in our <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor guide</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Can powered roller conveyors accumulate product?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Standard continuous-run powered rollers do not accumulate. Zone controls (pneumatic belt lifters for BDLR, clutch/brake for line-shaft) enable <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> provide built-in ZPA without pneumatic add-ons.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What speed range do powered rollers support?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR: 50-200 FPM. Line-shaft: 30-180 FPM. CDLR: 20-120 FPM. Speed is controlled by the drive motor and adjustable with a variable frequency drive (VFD). Most packaging applications run 60-150 FPM.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What maintenance do powered roller conveyors require?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR needs periodic belt tension, tracking, and replacement. Line-shaft needs O-ring replacement (5-10% annually in continuous duty). CDLR needs chain lubrication and periodic chain/sprocket replacement. All types need motor and gearbox lubrication per manufacturer schedule. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> eliminate belts, O-rings, and chains entirely.</p>

</div>
</div>
